            </div>

            <div class="key-finding">
                <strong>Equity Finding:</strong> High-income countries bear a significantly higher tax burden on fuel ({stats['tax_by_income']['High']}%) compared to low-income countries ({stats['tax_by_income']['Low']}%). Paradoxically, high-income countries also pay higher absolute fuel prices (${stats['price_by_income']['High']}/L vs ${stats['price_by_income']['Low']}/L), reflecting the removal of subsidies and market-based pricing. However, when expressed as a proportion of income, fuel remains more affordable in high-income nations.
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="findings">
            <h2>6. Key Findings & Policy Implications</h2>

            <h3>Executive Summary: Key Findings</h3>

            <div class="key-finding">
                <strong>1. Brent-to-Retail Correlation is Weak but Context-Dependent</strong><br>
                The average correlation between Brent Crude and retail petrol prices is only <strong>r = {stats['avg_corr_petrol']}</strong>, but this varies dramatically by year. During the 2020 COVID-19 shock, correlation peaked at <strong>r = {stats['brent_petrol_corr_by_year']['2020']}</strong> as markets moved in unison. Post-pandemic policy divergence reduced correlation to <strong>r = {stats['brent_petrol_corr_by_year']['2025']}</strong> by 2025. <em> Policymakers should not assume that crude price changes translate directly into retail price changes.</em>
            </div>

            <div class="key-finding">
                <strong>2. Subsidy Frameworks Show Clear Price Dampening Effects</strong><br>
                High-subsidy countries demonstrate a <strong>{stats['subsidy_dampening_effect']:.1f}% lower</strong> average petrol price compared to low-subsidy countries, with volatility reduced by a factor of ~5 (σ = {stats['subsidy_volatility']['High']} vs σ = {stats['subsidy_volatility']['Low']}). Very High subsidy countries maintain prices below $0.15/L on average. However, this protection comes at significant fiscal cost and is most sustainable in resource-rich nations.
            </div>

            <div class="key-finding">
                <strong>3. Regional Price Inequality is Persistent and Growing</strong><br>
                The gap between the highest-paying region (Europe, ${stats['regional_avg']['mean']['Europe']}/L) and lowest-paying region (Middle East, ${stats['regional_avg']['mean']['Middle East']}/L) represents a <strong>3:1 price ratio</strong>. This regional spread has grown from <strong>${stats['yearly_spreads']['2020']}</strong> in 2020 to <strong>${stats['yearly_spreads']['2026']}</strong> in 2026—a 78% increase in inequality. Energy-poor regions face structurally higher burdens.
            </div>

            <div class="key-finding">
                <strong>4. High-Income Countries Tax More, Low-Income Countries Subsidize More</strong><br>
                High-income nations collect an average of <strong>{stats['tax_by_income']['High']}%</strong> of fuel prices as tax, while low-income nations collect only <strong>{stats['tax_by_income']['Low']}%</strong>. This reflects different fiscal policy paradigms: high-income countries rely on fuel taxation for revenue, while low-income countries use subsidies to protect citizens from global price shocks.
            </div>

            <div class="key-finding">
                <strong>5. The 2022 Energy Crisis Caused a Structural Break in Pricing</strong><br>
                Brent Crude jumped from ${stats['brent_2020']}/bbl (2020) to ${stats['brent_2022']}/bbl (2022)—a <strong>69% increase</strong>. Retail petrol followed with a <strong>69% increase</strong> (${stats['petrol_2020']} to ${stats['petrol_2022']}/L), but the pass-through was uneven. Countries with high subsidies managed to limit increases to 20-30%, while unsubsidized markets absorbed the full shock.
            </div>

            <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>

            <ol style="margin-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin: 15px 0;">
                    <strong>Design Tiered Subsidy Systems:</strong> Rather than binary high/low subsidies, implement progressive subsidy tiers that protect low-income households while allowing market pricing for higher consumption levels. This preserves price stability for vulnerable populations without unlimited fiscal exposure.
                </li>
                <li style="margin: 15px 0;">
                    <strong>Build Volatility Stabilization Funds:</strong> During low-crude-price periods, capture windfall revenues into stabilization funds that can subsidize prices during spikes. This avoids the fiscal whiplash of sudden subsidy expansions.
                </li>
                <li style="margin: 15px 0;">
                    <strong>Calibrate Tax Policy Proactively:</strong> Given the high tax-to-price ratio in high-income countries ({stats['tax_by_income']['High']}%), there is room to temporarily reduce fuel taxes during energy crises to cushion consumer impact—without eliminating taxes entirely.
                </li>
                <li style="margin: 15px 0;">
                    <strong>Address Regional Inequality Through Trade Agreements:</strong> Persistent 3:1 regional price ratios suggest structural barriers to price arbitrage. Regional energy trade agreements could help equalize access to affordable fuel.
                </li>
                <li style="margin: 15px 0;">
                    <strong>Monitor Subsidy Sustainability Metrics:</strong> With very-high-subsidy countries maintaining sub-$0.15/L prices, long-term fiscal sustainability should be assessed. International institutions should develop subsidy health indicators.
                </li>
            </ol>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Analytical Caveat:</strong> This analysis is based on the available dataset from January 2020 to April 2026. Tax percentage data shows notable week-to-week variability within countries, suggesting potential methodological differences in estimation. Subsidy levels are categorical (High, Low, Medium, Very High) rather than continuous, limiting the precision of subsidy-quantity analyses.
            </div>
        </section>

        <div class="footer">
            <p><strong>Report Generated:</strong> April 2026 | <strong>Data Source:</strong> Global Fuel Prices Dataset (84 countries, 2020-2026)</p>
            <p>This analysis was prepared for energy policy analysts evaluating subsidy design, tax calibration, and price stabilization strategies.</p>
